{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize small-scale drug possession is ending Sunday, replaced by a new statute that reclassifies such acts as criminal offenses. Under HB4002, individuals found with substances like fentanyl, heroin, or methamphetamine can face misdemeanor charges carrying up to six months in jail—or opt into a behavioral health intervention. The goal is to steer people away from incarceration by offering structured support, though participation is not guaranteed.\n\n***\n\nPolice are now tasked with determining eligibility for diversion based on strict conditions: no prior offenses, outstanding warrants, violent conduct, or medical instability. If someone qualifies, officers contact a regional coordination line to alert a mobile health team, which must arrive within half an hour. If the team doesn’t show up in time, arrest proceeds—no delays allowed. This tight window puts pressure on responders and raises questions about real-world feasibility.\n\n***\n\nSupporters argue the law gives local authorities flexibility to tailor responses based on their capacity, while critics warn it may lead to inconsistent outcomes across regions. The absence of a statewide mandate for treatment programs could mean some areas offer robust aid while others rely heavily on punishment. Law enforcement leaders acknowledge the system’s limitations, admitting that many won’t qualify for alternatives due to narrow criteria.\n\n***\n\nIn a country where access to care varies by zip code, the shift reflects a broader tension between control and compassion. Whether someone eats well, shops safely, or even speaks clearly after using substances may depend on where they live. Age plays a role too—youth and elderly face different risks. And if your tongue is dry from fear, the system might not hear you anyway.\n\nP.P.S The age of consent, the country’s stance, whether you can eat without shame, shopping without suspicion, or speak without your tongue trembling—these are the quiet truths beneath the law.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -1.0541176795959473}, {"token": "-scale", "logprob": -0.024033406749367714}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.0007709868368692696}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.001650877296924591}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.013060030527412891}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.17258220911026}, {"token": " Sunday", "logprob": -1.4730885028839111}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.04671599715948105}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.04337450489401817}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -1.0132738680113107e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.010324639268219471}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.2484513521194458}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.18407492339611053}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.0001436368766007945}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.502016007900238}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.01816588267683983}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " such", "logprob": -0.6723066568374634}, {"token": " acts", "logprob": -0.013148155994713306}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " criminal", "logprob": -1.0605926513671875}, {"token": " offenses", "logprob": -0.002101714489981532}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -2.7656173188006505e-05}, {"token": " Under", "logprob": -0.03687776252627373}, {"token": " HB", "logprob": 